section From_C7_c7_body
/-! # C7 — the six numeric facts of C6 / X-L7, as Lean theorems

GENERATED by `gen_c7.py`; edit that, not this file.  `gen_c7.py --check` fails on drift.

`Zeta2SupScheme.routeB_ingredients` and `Zeta2L7Assembly.routeB_l7_rate` take six numeric
hypotheses at route B's certified saddle.  Until this row they lived in exact-rational
Python and were therefore outside the kernel-checked chain.  §5 proves all six and §6
feeds them to the LANDED theorem, so the kernel — not the eye — checks that what is
proved here is what that theorem assumes.

Two structural facts make this small (both are algebra, not machinery):

* **The arctan collapse.**  `g'(w) = π - Σ arctan(w/qᵢ) + arctan(w/D)` and every argument
  is rational, so `Real.arctan_add` folds the eight arctans into `arctan R + Nπ` with `R`
  rational — here `N = 1` at all four `w`, so `g'(w) = -arctan(R(w))` EXACTLY.  The two
  bracket signs are then the rational facts `R(w*-r) ≤ 0`, `R(w*+r) ≥ 0`: infinite
  precision, no enclosure, and `Real.pi` never appears.
* **π cancels out of `g`.**  Identically `g(w) = c + ½(Σ qᵢ log(qᵢ²+w²) - D log(D²+w²))
  + w·g'(w)` (`phase_eq_logpart`), so the only enclosures C7 needs are LOGS at rationals.

Mathlib has no `Real.arctan` enclosure, no `Real.log` enclosure at a general rational, no
`norm_num` extension for either and no interval arithmetic — but it does have
`Real.abs_log_sub_add_sum_range_le` (the `log (1-x)` Taylor remainder), which is all §2
needs, and `Real.arctan_add` + `Real.le_tan`, which is all §1 and §3 need.
-/

