/-! # 2B0.E — route B's L7 rate, HYPOTHESIS-FREE for the σ̃ line integral

This row proves nothing new.  It executes the composition of two rows that each discharged a
complementary half of `Zeta2L7Assembly.routeB_l7_rate`'s hypotheses, in two different files
that had never met (`skills/LEAN.md` §3: proving pieces "with the others as hypotheses" is not
composing, and it failed on casts BOTH times it was skipped in this program):

* **C7** (`../zeta2_c7_enclosures/`) — `Zeta2C7.routeB_l7_rate_numeric` applies the real
  `routeB_l7_rate` with the **six numeric facts** discharged (`hbl`, `hbr`, `hε₀`, `hG`, `h3`,
  `hs3`), leaving `hrep` as its only hypothesis.
* **M5** (`../zeta2_l7_middle/`) — `L7MidM5.routeB_l7_rate_hrep_discharged` applies the real
  `routeB_l7_rate` with **`hrep`** discharged, leaving those same six.

Put together the hypotheses are gone.  `routeB_l7_rate_sigmaTilde` below is that statement,
and it applies the LANDED `Zeta2L7Assembly.routeB_l7_rate` (through M5's composition), not a
restatement of it.

## WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CLAIM — read the scope before quoting the result

The sequence bounded here is `L7MidM5.rLine`, the **real part of M5's §1 line integral** at the σ̃
contour, `rLine n = ((1/2π) ∫_{ℝ} H_n(−σ̃n − ½ + is) ds).re`.  It is **NOT** the chain's remainder
`r_n = q_n·ζ(2) − p_n`.

Connecting the two is *separate* work that has not landed: Lemma 9's contour move (M6) plus the
L1/A2 arithmetic identification of the line integral with `q_n ζ(2) − p_n`.  So:

* **Claimed:** route B's L7 rate holds unconditionally for the σ̃ line integral.
* **NOT claimed:** that L7 is closed, or anything at all about the chain's `r_n`.

`L7MidM5.routeB_l7_rate_of_le_norm` is the interface that row will use — it takes any real
sequence dominated by `‖rIntC n‖` — so nothing here has to be reopened when it lands.

## THE COMPOSITION COST ZERO ITERATIONS, AND THE FRICTION I PREDICTED WAS NOT REAL

Worth recording precisely, because the prediction was wrong in the direction `LEAN.md` §9 warns
about — a confident claim about work already done.

`κ_Π` **is** defined twice in the chain, identically and independently: `Zeta2C7.kappaPi` (in
`c7_body.lean`, generated by `gen_c7.py`) and `L7MidM123.kappaPi` (in `m123_glue.lean`).  Same
right-hand side, two constants.  C7's `c7_hG` / `c7_h3` are stated at its own; M5's `hG` / `h3`
slots expect M123's.  I predicted that unification would run at *reducible* transparency, fail
to unfold two plain `def`s, and force an explicit bridge — exactly the cast/indexing class §3
says to expect — and wrote `kappaPi_agree` before testing whether it was needed.

**It was not.**  Feeding `Zeta2C7.c7_hG` / `Zeta2C7.c7_h3` straight into M5's slots, with the
bridge removed, elaborates green and produces a clean receipt for
`routeB_l7_rate_sigmaTilde` (measured 2026-09-08; the falsifier is `falsify_bridge.sh`, and it
carries a control confirming the substitution was not a no-op).  The final `isDefEq` that
checks an application argument against its expected type runs at DEFAULT transparency, so it
unfolds both `def`s and sees one term.  The whole composition went through on the first run.

`kappaPi_agree` is therefore **hygiene, not glue**, and it is kept for a different reason than
the one it was written for: it is a rung-0 pin on the duplicate.  If either row's `κ_Π` is ever
re-derived and the two drift apart, `rfl` reds *here*, on one line that says what broke, rather
than surfacing as an unification failure inside a 4666-line file.  `c7_hG_at_M123` /
`c7_h3_at_M123` are kept for the same reason and are used by the capstone below, so the pin is
load-bearing in the file even though the bridge is not load-bearing in the mathematics.

The duplicate itself is the genuine architectural finding, and it is worth removing at the
source: one of the two rows should consume the other's definition rather than restate it.
-/

